<Base title="Secondary Assists: Measuring Ball Movement | Bence Supola"><div class="container"><div class="case-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Case study 02 · Event extraction / Basketball analytics</div><h1>Does the Pass Before the Assist Matter?</h1><p class="lead">Measuring whether secondary assists reveal better ball movement, more open shooters and higher-quality scoring opportunities.</p><div class="skills"><span class="skill">Event extraction</span><span class="skill">Multi-source alignment</span><span class="skill">Logistic regression</span><span class="skill">Passing-pattern analysis</span></div></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>18,834</strong><span>Potential secondary assists extracted</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>0.945</strong><span>Pass-detection F1 in manual validation</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>0.955</strong><span>Shot-detection F1 in manual validation</span></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>The real research question</h2><p>Basketball statistics record assists, but the pass <em>before</em> the assist has received much less attention. We investigated whether secondary-assisted possessions systematically lead to more valuable shooting situations, especially open corner threes, and whether secondary assists can serve as a practical indicator of effective ball movement.</p><p>That question cannot be answered by counting passes alone. We needed to know where pass sequences began and ended, whether a shot followed, where the nearest defender stood, and how the resulting shot quality compared with other offensive actions.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>First obstacle: tracking coordinates are not event labels</h2><p>The publicly available 2015–16 SportVU data contain high-frequency positions, while play-by-play events have coarser timing and do not enumerate every pass. We developed a rule-based extractor to identify the ball handler, same-team possession changes and shot starts, then aligned the derived events with play-by-play information.</p><p>The implementation included practical safeguards: identifying the possessing team using shot-clock information, requiring a minimum 0.4-second player possession to reduce false pass detections, and tracing ball trajectories toward the basket to locate shot initiation more precisely than play-by-play timestamps. These choices introduced known trade-offs, including reduced sensitivity to very short touch passes.</p><p>Validation against manually annotated segments from six previously unseen games produced F1 scores of <strong>0.945 for passes</strong> and <strong>0.955 for shots</strong>. These numbers measure event extraction, not the accuracy of the later shot-quality model.</p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/secondary-pass-detection.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/secondary-pass-detection.png" alt="Ball tracking points with detected passing trajectories highlighted in different colors." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>From positions to events.</strong> Unpublished diagnostic visualization: colored trajectory segments mark detected passes; black points represent other recorded ball positions. This illustrates the event-extraction problem, not a prediction of future passes.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>Second obstacle: defining a measurable secondary assist</h2><p>Official assist conventions involve judgment and differ from a fully reproducible event definition. For the analysis we applied a consistent <strong>two-second possession window</strong> to both the final assist and the preceding secondary assist, without imposing a dribble-count rule. This made it possible to identify potential secondary assists and compare passing patterns consistently.</p><p>The study extracted <strong>18,834 potential secondary assists</strong> from more than 500 NBA games. These are events under the paper’s operational definition, not a claim that every one would be credited as an official NBA secondary assist.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>Third obstacle: separating shot quality from small-sample luck</h2><p>Simply ranking passing patterns by made shots would overvalue sequences with very few attempts. We divided the court into meaningful regions and modeled shot quality from <strong>basket distance</strong> and <strong>nearest-defender distance</strong>. Logistic regression provided an initial shot-outcome score, which was calibrated to an interpretable expected-points scale.</p><p>We then compared unassisted, assisted and secondary-assisted shots, examined passing locations, and investigated whether defender proximity or the slightly shorter corner-three distance better explained accuracy. This was observational analysis: associations in the dataset do not establish that adding a secondary assist would cause a better shot.</p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/secondary-inside-out.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/secondary-inside-out.png" alt="Unpublished court visualization of spatial links and passing positions in an inside-out pattern." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>Inside-out passing geometry.</strong> Unpublished exploratory plot from the research data. It illustrates the spatial-pattern analysis; colors are not assigned unverified semantic labels.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>What we found — and what it means</h2><p>Secondary-assisted shots were especially prominent in high-value perimeter areas. In the corner-three category, the paper reports <strong>1.173 average points per possession</strong> for secondary-assisted shots versus <strong>1.102</strong> for only-assisted and <strong>1.055</strong> for unassisted shots in the analyzed sample. The mean nearest-defender distance was also greater for secondary-assisted corner threes.</p><p>Passing patterns routed through central areas, including the high post, featured among the highest modeled expected-points sequences ending in the corners. These findings support secondary assists as a potentially useful <strong>team-level ball-movement KPI</strong>, not a universal individual skill rating or proof of a causal advantage.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>My role and scope</h2><p>I implemented the programming and analytical workflow underpinning this collaborative research. The study design, scientific interpretation and publication were developed with my coauthors.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/24748668.2022.2039090">Read the peer-reviewed paper ↗</a> · <a href="/#work">Back to selected work</a></p></div></div></Base>
